{"name":"Quartersawn Oak","hex":"#85695b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"quartersawn","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["past participle of quartersaw"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/quartersawn"},{"term":"oak","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Noakhali."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A deciduous tree with distinctive deeply lobed leaves, acorns, and notably strong wood, typically of England and northeastern North America, included in genus Quercus.","The wood of the oak."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/oak"}],"entries":[{"title":"Quarter sawing","blocks":[{"text":"Quarter sawing or quartersawing is a woodworking process that produces quarter-sawn or quarter-cut boards in the rip cutting of logs into lumber. The resulting lumber can also be called radially-sawn or simply quartered. There is widespread confusion between the terms rift sawn and quarter sawn, with the terms defined both with opposite meanings and as synonyms."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Quartersawing-16-19-inches.jpg/330px-Quartersawing-16-19-inches.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quarter_sawing"},{"title":"Oak","blocks":[{"text":"An oak is a hardwood tree or shrub in the genus Quercus of the beech family. They have spirally arranged leaves, often with lobed edges, and a nut called an acorn, borne within a cup. The genus is widely distributed in the Northern Hemisphere, with some 475 species, both deciduous and evergreen. Fossil oaks date back to the Middle Eocene. Molecular phylogeny shows that the genus is divided into Old World and New World clades, but many oak species hybridise freely, making the genus's history difficult to resolve."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Quercus_robur.jpg/330px-Quercus_robur.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oak"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Quartersawing-over-19-inches.jpg/330px-Quartersawing-over-19-inches.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Quartersawing-over-19-inches.jpg/330px-Quartersawing-over-19-inches.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A method for logs over 19 in (48 cm)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AQuartersawing-over-19-inches.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Cedrus_wood.jpg/330px-Cedrus_wood.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Cedrus_wood.jpg/330px-Cedrus_wood.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"quarter sawn surface, showing ray fleck (modest in this case)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACedrus_wood.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Brockwell_Oak_%289%29.jpg/120px-Brockwell_Oak_%289%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Brockwell_Oak_%289%29.jpg/250px-Brockwell_Oak_%289%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Brockwell_Oak_%289%29.jpg/120px-Brockwell_Oak_%289%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABrockwell_Oak_(9).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Quercus_lusitanica_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-253.jpg/120px-Quercus_lusitanica_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen